Armor isn't about avoiding hits. Hits happen, that's the always-hit rule in action. Armor is what you wear and what you have been through: boiled leather, rings of steel, a breastplate that has already turned one blade. It is a choice about weight, slots, donning time, and what it says about you. What keeps you standing is the steel you wear and the drills you have run, working together. Damage Reduction comes from your armor and from your talents.

Shields are different. A shield is not a passive number on your sheet, it is an active defense, something you put in the way of a blow. It gives you a reaction that downgrades an incoming hit. Wear your armor. Use your shield. Choose wisely.

#pagebreak()

== How Damage Reduction Works

When you take physical damage, subtract your Damage Reduction (DR) from the damage value. The remainder is what you actually lose from your HP. A hit always lands for at least 1 damage, no matter how much DR you have. Simple.

Your DR is your armor's DR plus what your talents add. Armor's DR is set by its weight class: light 1, medium 2, heavy 3. Heavier armor is heavier in every sense, and in the kit builder it costs more points. So the steel you wear and the training you have done stack, and a well-armored veteran is harder to hurt than either alone.

*There is a ceiling.* Your total DR cannot exceed 3 at Novice, 4 at Adept, or 6 at Master. A hit must always be able to land for something, and the three tiers of a damage band have to stay distinct at every level. If DR could climb past that ceiling the roll would stop carrying information, and light, medium, and heavy armor would stop being three different choices. Armor and training stack up to the ceiling, and no further.

*Example:* Kael wears light armour, so his armour grants DR 1. He has Iron Skin at Novice rank, which adds +2. His total DR is 3. A goblin's scimitar hits him for Weak damage (4). 4 - 3 = 1, so Kael takes 1 damage. When the same goblin lands a Standard hit (6), Kael takes 3 instead of 6.

